[{:db/id 17592186198270 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-787ecee8-f1f0-5525-9f42-a25a7295c8af" "question-c8147626-87d7-56ca-be09-d1e1490d794e"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186198271 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-a6b24cf2-2976-579e-8d0e-dd268f3c65a7" "question-d8b98aed-4075-5dac-9091-d486170b806a"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-787ecee8-f1f0-5525-9f42-a25a7295c8af" :question/id #uuid "787ecee8-f1f0-5525-9f42-a25a7295c8af" :question/math-academy-id "q-181669" :question/problem "Which of the following sets are infinite?\n\n- $A={n\\in Z\\,:\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2n\\pi )=0}$\n- $B={q\\in Q\\,:\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2q\\pi )=0}$\n- $C={x\\in R\\,:\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x\\pi )=0}$" :question/worked-solution "The cardinality of a finite set is the number of elements contained within it. A set is infinite if it has infinitely many elements.\n\nWe start by finding the solutions to the equation $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x\\pi )=0$ for $x\\in R:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x\\pi  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}+m\\pi  \\\\ x & =\\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{m}{2},\\,m\\in Z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our sets:\n\n- The set $A$ is finite. There are no values of $m\\in Z$ such that $\\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{m}{2}\\in Z.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nA={}.\n$$\n\nConsequently, $|A|=0,$ which is finite.\n\n- The set $B$ is infinite. Listing the elements of $B$ for different values of $m,$ we have\n\n$$\nB={…,-\\frac{3}{4},-\\frac{1}{4},\\frac{1}{4},\\frac{3}{4},…}.\n$$\n\n- The set $C$ is infinite. Since $B$ is infinite, and $B⊆C,$ it follows that $C$ is infinite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$B$ and $C$ only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-9ef252b3-0c31-53e5-9944-1acf64287b65"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-9ef252b3-0c31-53e5-9944-1acf64287b65" :answer-field/id #uuid "9ef252b3-0c31-53e5-9944-1acf64287b65" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-faa7110f-20ad-5b9f-af55-e84b316f4f1f" :answer-field/choices ["answer-a0d2d1d1-0842-520f-b8fa-9b61ab89e6fd" "answer-486b0493-eb45-55ed-82f5-c032db770e60" "answer-3c6dea98-f9b5-5483-ad34-13ce6f559a2d" "answer-faa7110f-20ad-5b9f-af55-e84b316f4f1f" "answer-e9f92ab3-81aa-5ff4-8365-5163fc68e5f5"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a0d2d1d1-0842-520f-b8fa-9b61ab89e6fd" :answer/id #uuid "a0d2d1d1-0842-520f-b8fa-9b61ab89e6fd" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$A$ and $B$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-486b0493-eb45-55ed-82f5-c032db770e60" :answer/id #uuid "486b0493-eb45-55ed-82f5-c032db770e60" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$A$ and $C$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3c6dea98-f9b5-5483-ad34-13ce6f559a2d" :answer/id #uuid "3c6dea98-f9b5-5483-ad34-13ce6f559a2d" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$A$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-faa7110f-20ad-5b9f-af55-e84b316f4f1f" :answer/id #uuid "faa7110f-20ad-5b9f-af55-e84b316f4f1f" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$B$ and $C$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e9f92ab3-81aa-5ff4-8365-5163fc68e5f5" :answer/id #uuid "e9f92ab3-81aa-5ff4-8365-5163fc68e5f5" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$C$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186287717 :question/worked-solution "The cardinality of a finite set is the number of elements contained within it. A set is infinite if it has infinitely many elements.\n\nWe start by finding the solutions to the equation $\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{x\\pi }{2})=0$ for $x\\in R:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x\\pi }{2} & =m\\pi  \\\\ x & =2m,\\,m\\in Z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our sets:\n\n- The set $A$ is infinite. Listing the elements of $B$ for different values of $m,$ we have\n\n$$\nA={…,-2,0,2,4,…}.\n$$\n\n- The set $B$ is infinite. Since $A$ is infinite, and $A⊆B,$ it follows that $B$ is infinite.\n\n- The set $C$ is infinite. Since $B$ is infinite, and $B⊆C,$ it follows that $C$ is infinite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$A,$ $B,$ and $C.$\"" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-c8147626-87d7-56ca-be09-d1e1490d794e" :question/id #uuid "c8147626-87d7-56ca-be09-d1e1490d794e" :question/math-academy-id "q-181667" :question/problem "Which of the following sets are infinite?\n\n- $A={n\\in Z\\,:\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi }{2})=0}$\n- $B={q\\in Q\\,:\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{q\\pi }{2})=0}$\n- $C={x\\in R\\,:\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{x\\pi }{2})=0}$" :question/worked-solution "The cardinality of a finite set is the number of elements contained within it. A set is infinite if it has infinitely many elements.\n\nWe start by finding the solutions to the equation $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{x\\pi }{2})=0$ for $x\\in R:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x\\pi }{2} & =m\\pi  \\\\ x & =2m,\\,m\\in Z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our sets:\n\n- The set $A$ is infinite. Listing the elements of $B$ for different values of $m,$ we have\n\n$$\nA={…,-2,0,2,4,…}.\n$$\n\n- The set $B$ is infinite. Since $A$ is infinite, and $A⊆B,$ it follows that $B$ is infinite.\n\n- The set $C$ is infinite. Since $B$ is infinite, and $B⊆C,$ it follows that $C$ is infinite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$A,$ $B,$ and $C.$\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-21e37987-951e-54e4-a566-e39dd06571da"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-21e37987-951e-54e4-a566-e39dd06571da" :answer-field/id #uuid "21e37987-951e-54e4-a566-e39dd06571da" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-43cc4526-e59c-5a0d-b9a8-f705fb0bf7a9" :answer-field/choices ["answer-c84a5364-9750-5067-9737-5e4daa30aa9e" "answer-dde382cc-266f-5db0-88f1-85b4627ded80" "answer-43cc4526-e59c-5a0d-b9a8-f705fb0bf7a9" "answer-1137ea51-0849-5c10-9f4b-2be034d8e893" "answer-099f3a38-4da4-5649-a22d-eba7684b0cfe"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c84a5364-9750-5067-9737-5e4daa30aa9e" :answer/id #uuid "c84a5364-9750-5067-9737-5e4daa30aa9e" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$A$ and $B$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-dde382cc-266f-5db0-88f1-85b4627ded80" :answer/id #uuid "dde382cc-266f-5db0-88f1-85b4627ded80" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$A$ and $C$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-43cc4526-e59c-5a0d-b9a8-f705fb0bf7a9" :answer/id #uuid "43cc4526-e59c-5a0d-b9a8-f705fb0bf7a9" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$A,$ $B,$ and $C$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1137ea51-0849-5c10-9f4b-2be034d8e893" :answer/id #uuid "1137ea51-0849-5c10-9f4b-2be034d8e893" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$B$ and $C$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-099f3a38-4da4-5649-a22d-eba7684b0cfe" :answer/id #uuid "099f3a38-4da4-5649-a22d-eba7684b0cfe" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$C$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-a6b24cf2-2976-579e-8d0e-dd268f3c65a7" :question/id #uuid "a6b24cf2-2976-579e-8d0e-dd268f3c65a7" :question/math-academy-id "q-181641" :question/problem "Which of the following sets are infinite?\n\n- $A={{f}^{(n)}(x)=\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{n}}{\\text{d}{x}^{n}}({e}^{1-2x})\\,:\\,n\\in {N}_{0}}$\n- $B={{g}^{(n)}(x)=\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{n}}{\\text{d}{x}^{n}}(3{e}^{x})\\,:\\,n\\in {N}_{0}}$\n- $C={{h}^{(n)}(x)=\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{n}}{\\text{d}{x}^{n}}(4{e}^{-x})\\,:\\,n\\in {N}_{0}}$" :question/worked-solution "The cardinality of a finite set is the number of elements contained within it. A set is infinite if it has infinitely many elements.\n\n- The set $A$ is infinite. Listing the first few elements of our set, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{(0)}(x) & ={e}^{1-2x} \\\\ {f}^{(1)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{1-2x})=-2{e}^{1-2x} \\\\ {f}^{(2)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-2{e}^{1-2x})=4{e}^{1-2x} \\\\ {f}^{(3)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(4{e}^{1-2x})=-8{e}^{1-2x} \\\\ ⋮\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we can write our set as follows:\n\n$$\nA={{e}^{1-2x},-2{e}^{1-2x},4{e}^{1-2x},-8{e}^{1-2x},…}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $A$ is infinite.\n\n- The set $B$ is finite. Listing the first few elements of our set, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{(0)}(x) & =3{e}^{x} \\\\ {g}^{(1)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3{e}^{x})=3{e}^{x} \\\\ {g}^{(2)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3{e}^{x})=3{e}^{x} \\\\ {g}^{(3)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3{e}^{x})=3{e}^{x} \\\\ ⋮\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, $B$ contains only one element:\n\n$$\nB={3{e}^{x}}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $|B|=1,$ which is finite.\n\n- The set $C$ is finite. Listing the first few elements of our set, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}^{(0)}(x) & =4{e}^{-x} \\\\ {h}^{(1)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(4{e}^{-x})=-4{e}^{-x} \\\\ {h}^{(2)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-4{e}^{-x})=4{e}^{-x} \\\\ {h}^{(3)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(4{e}^{-x})=-4{e}^{-x} \\\\ ⋮\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, $C$ contains only two elements:\n\n$$\nC={4{e}^{-x},-4{e}^{-x}}.\n$$\n\nConsequently, $|C|=2,$ which is finite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$A$ only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-981d986a-6dfa-540d-944a-7be3c24f9b94"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-981d986a-6dfa-540d-944a-7be3c24f9b94" :answer-field/id #uuid "981d986a-6dfa-540d-944a-7be3c24f9b94" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-6907e3ce-e2f4-5289-974d-b7b687315b11" :answer-field/choices ["answer-a0223240-2eb5-5bc2-b389-218324c528fe" "answer-6907e3ce-e2f4-5289-974d-b7b687315b11" "answer-15ca3f93-b276-5422-a4f0-a2e4f89be083" "answer-1cb5e67c-cb84-5412-9a3e-61b4099aeabd" "answer-65e1ba83-f0a5-5cc9-8f4a-3ea942a47e04"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a0223240-2eb5-5bc2-b389-218324c528fe" :answer/id #uuid "a0223240-2eb5-5bc2-b389-218324c528fe" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$A$ and $B$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6907e3ce-e2f4-5289-974d-b7b687315b11" :answer/id #uuid "6907e3ce-e2f4-5289-974d-b7b687315b11" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$A$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-15ca3f93-b276-5422-a4f0-a2e4f89be083" :answer/id #uuid "15ca3f93-b276-5422-a4f0-a2e4f89be083" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$B$ and $C$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1cb5e67c-cb84-5412-9a3e-61b4099aeabd" :answer/id #uuid "1cb5e67c-cb84-5412-9a3e-61b4099aeabd" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$B$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-65e1ba83-f0a5-5cc9-8f4a-3ea942a47e04" :answer/id #uuid "65e1ba83-f0a5-5cc9-8f4a-3ea942a47e04" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$C$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-d8b98aed-4075-5dac-9091-d486170b806a" :question/id #uuid "d8b98aed-4075-5dac-9091-d486170b806a" :question/math-academy-id "q-181706" :question/problem "Which of the following sets are infinite?\n\n- $A={{f}^{(n)}(x)=\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{n}}{\\text{d}{x}^{n}}(\\sqrt[5]{{x}^{2}})\\,:\\,n\\in {N}_{0}}$\n- $B={{g}^{(n)}(x)=\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{n}}{\\text{d}{x}^{n}}(-\\sqrt{x})\\,:\\,n\\in {N}_{0}}$\n- $C={{h}^{(n)}(x)=\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{n}}{\\text{d}{x}^{n}}(\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})\\,:\\,n\\in {N}_{0}}$" :question/worked-solution "The cardinality of a finite set is the number of elements contained within it. A set is infinite if it has infinitely many elements.\n\n- The set $A$ is infinite. Listing the first few elements of our set, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{(0)}(x) & =\\sqrt[5]{{x}^{2}} \\\\ {f}^{(1)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\sqrt[5]{{x}^{2}})=\\frac{2}{5\\sqrt[5]{{x}^{3}}} \\\\ {f}^{(2)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{2}{5\\sqrt[3]{{x}^{2}}})=-\\frac{6}{25\\sqrt[5]{{x}^{8}}} \\\\ ⋮\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we can write our set as follows:\n\n$$\nA={\\sqrt[5]{{x}^{2}},\\frac{2}{5\\sqrt[5]{{x}^{3}}},-\\frac{6}{25\\sqrt[5]{{x}^{8}}},…}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $A$ is infinite.\n\n- The set $B$ is also infinite. Similar to the previous case, listing the first few elements of our set, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{(0)}(x) & =-\\sqrt{x} \\\\ {g}^{(1)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-\\sqrt{x})=-\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}} \\\\ {g}^{(2)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}})=\\frac{1}{4\\sqrt{{x}^{3}}} \\\\  & ⋮\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we can write our set as follows:\n\n$$\nB={-\\sqrt{x},-\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}},\\frac{1}{4\\sqrt{{x}^{3}}},…}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $B$ is infinite.\n\n- The set $C$ is also infinite. Listing a few elements of our set, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}^{(0)}(x) & =\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ {h}^{(1)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})=-\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}} \\\\ {h}^{(2)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}})=\\frac{6}{{x}^{4}} \\\\  & ⋮\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we can write our set as follows:\n\n$$\nC={\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}},-\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}},\\frac{6}{{x}^{4}},…}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $C$ is infinite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$A,$ $B,$ and $C.$\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-5beabf59-4d47-5db3-877d-e192dae60847"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-5beabf59-4d47-5db3-877d-e192dae60847" :answer-field/id #uuid "5beabf59-4d47-5db3-877d-e192dae60847" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-1488dfbe-7dda-5765-aaae-fecd9ca9caa5" :answer-field/choices ["answer-872b5930-b555-5b00-977e-8ecaeb8ec451" "answer-c9a4b8b0-70fb-5140-bd89-b7cb08f1ab9f" "answer-1488dfbe-7dda-5765-aaae-fecd9ca9caa5" "answer-d21b5f4e-d145-5b7f-b843-065f3a5904e0" "answer-671c41b3-d8d4-5559-b179-e68f0f12a414"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-872b5930-b555-5b00-977e-8ecaeb8ec451" :answer/id #uuid "872b5930-b555-5b00-977e-8ecaeb8ec451" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$A$ and $B$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c9a4b8b0-70fb-5140-bd89-b7cb08f1ab9f" :answer/id #uuid "c9a4b8b0-70fb-5140-bd89-b7cb08f1ab9f" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$A$ and $C$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1488dfbe-7dda-5765-aaae-fecd9ca9caa5" :answer/id #uuid "1488dfbe-7dda-5765-aaae-fecd9ca9caa5" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$A,$ $B,$ and $C$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d21b5f4e-d145-5b7f-b843-065f3a5904e0" :answer/id #uuid "d21b5f4e-d145-5b7f-b843-065f3a5904e0" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$B$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-671c41b3-d8d4-5559-b179-e68f0f12a414" :answer/id #uuid "671c41b3-d8d4-5559-b179-e68f0f12a414" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$C$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186199555 :tutorial/content "Recall that a set is **finite** if it contains a finite number of elements and is **infinite** if it has infinitely many elements.\n\nIn this lesson, we'll determine whether a set given in set-builder notation is finite or infinite.\n\nFor example, let's determine which of the following sets are finite and which are infinite:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A={n\\in Z\\,:\\,|2n|\\le 8},\\,B={q\\in Q\\,:\\,|2q|\\le 8},\\,C={x\\in R\\,:\\,|2x|\\le 8}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that all three sets are defined using a conditional definition with the same restricting inequality defined over different universal sets ($\\mathbb{Z},$ $\\mathbb{Q},$ and $\\mathbb{R}$).\n\nFirstly, since $\\mathbb Z\\subset \\mathbb Q \\subset \\mathbb R,$ we start by solving the restricting inequality $|2x| \\leq 8$ for $x\\in \\mathbb R{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|2x|\\le 8 \\\\ 2|x|\\le 8 \\\\ |x|\\le 4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis inequality has the solution\n\n$$\n-4 \\leq x \\leq 4.\n$$\n\nFor convenience, we'll express our solution in interval notation as $x\\in I,$ where $I=\\left[-4,4\\right].$ \n\nNow, before we analyze our sets, we need to state a couple of theorems:\n\n ** *Theorem 1* **: *Every real interval of nonzero length contains infinitely many rational numbers.*\n\n ** *Theorem 2* **: *If $X$ is infinite and $X\\subseteq Y,$ then $Y$ is infinite.*\n\nWe're now ready to determine whether $A, B,$ and $C$ are finite or infinite:\n\n- Set $A$ is finite. If $n \\in \\mathbb{Z},$ the solutions to the inequality $|2n| \\leq 8$ are all the *integers* that lie in the interval $I.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\nA = \\{0,\\pm 1, \\pm 2, \\pm 3, \\pm 4\\}.\n$$\n\nConsequently, $|A| = 9,$ which is finite.\n\n- Set $B$ is infinite. If $q \\in \\mathbb{Q},$ the solutions to $|2q| \\leq 8$ are all the *rational* numbers in the interval $I.$ Since any real interval of nonzero length contains infinitely many rational numbers (by Theorem 1), it follows that $B$ is infinite.\n\n- Set $C$ is infinite. Since $B$ is infinite, and $B \\subset C,$ it follows that $C$ is infinite (by Theorem 2).\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $A$ is finite, while $B$ and $C$ are infinite. \n\nLet's see some more examples." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186198531 :activity/expected-seconds 660.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]}]
